[
  {
    "sha": "3f221475081ff57397a749a9b18f366243043167",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVoU8JdoAKDNmMjIxNDc1MDgxZmY1NzM5N2E3NDlhOWIxOGYzNjYyNDMwNDMxNjc",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-12T14:44:52Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-12T14:44:52Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(post): refuse by name a post that does not serve its setup's machine kind\n\npost_serves was only the Setup pane's listing law; no posting path asked\nit, so a lathe setup given a milling post (Haas, or the default Fanuc)\nposted its turning ops as a milling program (G17, T M6, G43, S M3, Y\nmoves). A new door (posting_serves.rs) runs on every posting path -\npost_setup/post_script, post_mounts/post_mounts_script and\npost_per_op/post_per_op_script - before any NC text: a mount whose post\ndoes not serve its machine's kind is refused naming the setup, its ops,\nthe post and the kind, and nothing is written (all or nothing per\nprogram). Shipped posts are judged by registry name, a user's script by\nits declared capabilities mask; no post serves an EDM.\n\nLathe test fixtures that posted turning programs through the default\nFanuc mill post now select Fanuc Lathe; the cross-drill fixture mounts a\nmill-turn (a cross hole needs a live spindle).\n\nFixes #1232\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
